@teknium1 — wanted to flag this for your attention. I built an implementation for this (PR #2) that mines eval data from Claude Code, Copilot, and Hermes session history. 114 tests, validated against real session data on disk.

The motivation: the self-evolution loop is Hermes's killer differentiator, but there's a cold-start problem for new users who don't have golden datasets yet. This lets them bootstrap from their existing AI tool usage.

Happy to adjust the approach based on your feedback.

I implemented the falsifiable contract from the audit as a narrow draft against the author branch: https://github.com/diegokolling/hermes-agent-self-evolution/pull/1

Verified at author head `9b75351` / extension head `dd4230a`:
- `state.db` is opened with URI `mode=ro`, `PRAGMA query_only`, and a single metadata/message snapshot;
- no policy => hard failure before store or scorer access;
- exact session ID + source + resolved project-directory allowlists;
- disallowed/missing/unresolvable metadata returns before any `messages` query and before the LLM scorer;
- present SQLite is authoritative even when empty or unreadable, so no implicit legacy fallback;
- legacy JSON requires explicit opt-in;
- synthetic fixtures only, disposable `HOME`/`HERMES_HOME`: **168 passed**.

No medical-keyword heuristic was added; the boundary is explicit metadata authorization and fail-closed validation.

The three verified follow-up defects now have a narrow draft against the exact author branch: https://github.com/MaxFreedomPollard/hermes-agent-self-evolution/pull/2

Head `4656e38` adds only:
- `estimable=false` for empty/single/constant paired-bootstrap intervals (including decimal differences equal within floating-point noise), with `PairedContinuous.inconclusive=true` when the CI is not estimable;
- `BehavioralOutcome.measured` in serialized artifacts;
- a distinct yellow `NO_CHANGE` rendering instead of red `failed ... exit 0`.

Disposable HOME/HERMES_HOME only: focused 214/214, full 1653/1653, diff-check PASS. No real corpus/runtime access.

@serdaryamac81-lab Thanks for reading it closely.

Done in 2030ceb: `--probe-report -` streams the full report (every probe with discriminating / dead / broken / orphaned status plus baseline, lesion and candidate verdicts, and the unprobed-lesson list) to stderr; `--probe-report PATH` writes the same JSON to a file. The per-probe verdict lines were already printed during evolution and `lesson_probes.json` is saved in the output directory, so this adds an explicit sink rather than new information.

One small correction: the gate itself is on by default, not opt-in. It just costs nothing without a `SKILL.probes.jsonl` (zero agent calls; it only lists the unguarded lesson-like passages). The opt-in part is `--generate-probes`, which is the only thing that spends calls without a probe file.

Follow-up to my note above, since the skill_structure fix turned out to be one of several stacked bugs. With one valid SKILL.md, a golden dataset and stub optimizers (no LLM), `evolve()` on `main` fails all 9 call-site tests I wrote:

- skill_structure checks the bare body (this issue);
- `dspy.GEPA(max_steps=…)` raises, so every run silently uses MIPROv2 (#10);
- the evolved text is read from an attribute no optimizer writes, so the output is always the baseline (#141, #172);
- nested frontmatter (#119);
- any positive holdout delta is a "win", even with no text change or 3 examples (#135);
- deletions pass the gate (#200).

Every one of these already has a contributor fix. I put the smallest correct one per bug on a branch, cherry-picked with `-x` so authorship stays: #208 @feafel, #109 @solidstatecc, #5 @AbdelStark, #104 @jarrettj, #16 @austinmw, #136/#205/#206 @MaxFreedomPollard, #201 @Mrtcdq, #166 @trippyogi. I added one gap fix (the no-op check compared against the raw file) and the call-site tests.

Branch: https://github.com/kvnloo/hermes-agent-self-evolution/compare/main...fix/stabilize-evolution-pipeline

| | main `0a929e3` | branch `4d177fe` |
|---|---|---|
| full suite | 145 passed | 216 passed |
| call-site tests | 0/9 | 9/9 |

Undoing any single fix on the branch makes its test fail again. Many one-line duplicates (#50, #95, #97, #161) still fail at their heads, because growth then compares the full file with the bare body.

@teknium1, would you take this as one PR? It would close #11, #10, #119, #135, #141, #164 and their duplicates (about 57 issues and PRs). Or would you rather have it split per fix? Also @MaxFreedomPollard: #136 is included although you closed it in favour of #162. Say if you'd rather it stay out.

<sub>Prepared with an AI agent, reviewed by me; every result above was produced by running the tests.</sub>
